[{"category":"section","location":"#SoftDotMessagePassingRules","page":"Overview","text":"The SoftDot node and its rules: a dot product of two unknowns, softened by Gaussian noise so that every message is in closed form. Use it for a Bayesian linear regression, y ~ N(θᵀx, γ⁻¹) with the noise precision γ learnt, or wherever a dot product joins two Gaussian unknowns, which the deterministic dot product node of StandardMessagePassingRules cannot do in closed form. This site is a single page.\n\ninfo: Where these rules run\nReactiveMP runs these rules on a factor graph, which RxInfer builds from a GraphPPL model. The examples here call the rules directly, as a test does.","title":"SoftDotMessagePassingRules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Overview","text":"A deterministic node y = θᵀx with both θ and x unknown has no closed-form messages. SoftDot replaces the constraint by a Gaussian likelihood of precision γ around it. The softening makes it a stochastic node, a density over its variables.\n\nUnder variational message passing, the node then sends a normal message towards each of y, θ and x and a gamma message towards γ, all in closed form. It contributes an average energy to the Bethe free energy. A large γ, or a prior on γ concentrated at large values, brings it close to the deterministic dot product.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Definition","page":"Overview","text":"p(y mid θ x γ) = mathcalNleft(y mid θ^top x γ^-1right)\n= sqrtfracγ2π expleft(-fracγ2 left(y - θ^top xright)^2right)\n\nThe output y is normal around the dot product θᵀx, with variance γ⁻¹. y is a scalar; θ and x are both scalars or both vectors of the same length.","title":"Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"#Interfaces","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftDotMessagePassingRules, MessagePassingRulesBase, BayesBase, ExponentialFamily\nMessagePassingRulesBase.nodespec(SoftDot)\nNodeSpec: SoftDot (stochastic)\n  interfaces:        y, θ (aliases: theta), x, γ (aliases: gamma)\n  default algorithm: MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm()\n  static inputs:     none\n  defined:           src/node.jl:38\n\nThe node has four interfaces, two of them with an alias that a model may write instead of the Greek name. The node itself has the alias softdot.\n\nname alias meaning its rules read\ny  the result of the soft dot product, a scalar q(y) a univariate normal; under q(y, x), the message on y, a univariate normal\nθ theta the first factor q(θ) a normal, univariate or multivariate\nx  the second factor q(x) a normal, or a point mass for known regressors; under q(y, x), the message on x, a normal\nγ gamma the precision of the noise q(γ) a gamma, through its mean (and the mean of its logarithm for the energy)\n\nHere q(θ) is the marginal of θ. The rules towards θ and x return a normal in weighted-mean–precision form, univariate or multivariate as the factor is. The rule towards y returns a univariate normal and the rule towards γ a GammaShapeRate. Under the structured factorisation the joint q(y, x) is a multivariate normal over [y; x].","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Algorithm","page":"Overview","text":"SoftDot runs under DefaultAlgorithm, which takes no parameters; a model need not name it. The node declares no dependencies, so each rule takes the inputs of the default scheme.","title":"Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-rules","page":"Overview","text":"MessagePassingRulesBase.rule_coverage(SoftDot)\nRule coverage for SoftDot\n                 │ DefaultAlgorithm\n  → y            │ ✓×2             \n  → θ            │ ✓×2             \n  → x            │ ✓×2             \n  → γ            │ ✓×2             \n  average energy │ ✓×2             \n  q(y, x)        │ ✓               \n\nrule_coverage counts two rules per target, one per factorisation:\n\nthe mean field q(y)q(θ)q(x)q(γ), where every rule reads the marginals of the other interfaces;\nthe structured q(y, x)q(θ)q(γ), where the rules towards y and x read the other one's message and the rules towards θ and γ read the joint q(y, x).\n\nThe marginal rule q(y, x) computes that joint from the messages on y and x. There are two average energies, one per factorisation. There are no belief propagation rules.","title":"Supported rules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Overview","text":"The message towards γ under mean field is Γ(3/2, ⟨(y - θᵀx)²⟩ / 2). With every marginal N(1, 1), the expected square is E[y²] - 2E[y]E[θ]E[x] + E[θ²]E[x²] = 2 - 2 + 4:\n\n@call_message_update_rule(\n    node = SoftDot, target = :γ,\n    q = (y = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), θ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), x = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0)),\n)\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mRuleResult\u001b[22m  message of SoftDot towards :γ  \u001b[90m· variational\u001b[39m\n  \u001b[0m\u001b[1mSoftDot\u001b[22m\n    y  \u001b[35m┄┄▶  q\u001b[39m  ExponentialFamily.NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=1.0)\n    θ  \u001b[35m┄┄▶  q\u001b[39m  ExponentialFamily.NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=1.0)\n    x  \u001b[35m┄┄▶  q\u001b[39m  ExponentialFamily.NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=1.0)\n    γ  \u001b[32m\u001b[1m◀══  target\u001b[22m\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mExponentialFamily.GammaShapeRate{Float64}(a=1.5, b=2.0)\u001b[39m\n  result     ExponentialFamily.GammaShapeRate{Float64}(a=1.5, b=2.0)\n  logscale   \u001b[33mundefined: the message rule for SoftDot towards :γ under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm declares no `logscale`\u001b[39m\n  algorithm  DefaultAlgorithm()\n  rule       q[:y]::Any, q[:θ]::Any, q[:x]::Any  @ src/rules.jl:70\n\u001b[90m  and 1 other rule for this target\u001b[39m\n\n@call_message_update_rule runs the rule and draws the marginals it read. The result is Γ(1.5, 2.0), which getresult extracts:\n\njulia> using MessagePassingRulesBase, SoftDotMessagePassingRules, ExponentialFamily, BayesBase\n\njulia> q = (y = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), θ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), x = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0));\n\njulia> message = getresult(@call_message_update_rule(node = SoftDot, target = :γ, q = q));\n\njulia> shape(message) ≈ 1.5 && rate(message) ≈ 2.0\ntrue","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Limitations","page":"Overview","text":"Variational rules only: there is no belief propagation rule, so the node needs a factorisation, mean-field or q(y, x)q(θ)q(γ). It also needs initial marginals where the schedule reads them before they are computed.\nNo rules for other factorisations, such as a joint over θ and x or over y and θ.\ny is a scalar: a vector-valued output needs one node per component. The mean-field rules and energy check it, and that θ and x are both univariate or both multivariate, and throw a RuleInputError otherwise.\nThe rules take normal marginals and messages on y, θ and x and a gamma-like marginal on γ (anything with a mean, and a mean of the logarithm for the energy).","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Overview","text":"The node and its alias, the name an RxInfer model writes:","title":"API"},{"category":"module","location":"#SoftDotMessagePassingRules","page":"Overview","text":"SoftDotMessagePassingRules\n\nThe SoftDot node, y ~ N(θᵀx, γ⁻¹): the dot product of θ and x softened by Gaussian noise of precision γ, with its variational rules. Where the deterministic dot product node, dot in StandardMessagePassingRules, has no closed-form messages for two Gaussian factors, SoftDot's are all in closed form. It serves Bayesian linear regression, y ~ N(θᵀx, γ⁻¹) with the regressors x known, and bilinear models with both factors unknown.\n\nSoftDot, the node, and its alias softdot. It runs under DefaultAlgorithm, which a model need not name.\nIts rules are variational, under the mean-field q(y)q(θ)q(x)q(γ) or the structured q(y, x)q(θ)q(γ), with an average energy for each. There are no belief-propagation rules.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using MessagePassingRulesBase, ExponentialFamily, BayesBase\n\njulia> q_θ = MvNormalMeanCovariance([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]);\n\njulia> q_x = MvNormalMeanCovariance([3.0, 1.0], [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]);\n\njulia> result = @call_message_update_rule(node = SoftDot, target = :y, q = (θ = q_θ, x = q_x, γ = GammaShapeRate(2.0, 1.0)));\n\njulia> all(mean_precision(getresult(result)) .≈ (5.0, 2.0))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SoftDotMessagePassingRules"},{"category":"type","location":"#SoftDotMessagePassingRules.SoftDot","page":"Overview","text":"SoftDot\n\nThe SoftDot node, the density\n\np(y | θ, x, γ) = N(y | θᵀx, γ⁻¹),\n\na dot product of θ and x softened by Gaussian noise of precision γ. A stochastic node with the interfaces, in order:\n\ny, the result, a scalar;\nθ (alias theta), the first factor, a scalar or a vector;\nx, the second factor, of the same dimension as θ;\nγ (alias gamma), the precision of the noise, a positive scalar.\n\nIt runs under DefaultAlgorithm, which a model need not name. Its rules are variational and in closed form: every message reads the marginals of the other interfaces, under the mean-field q(y)q(θ)q(x)q(γ) or the structured q(y, x)q(θ)q(γ), where the rules towards y and x read the message on the other one and the joint q(y, x) has its own rule. The marginals on y, θ and x are normals and the one on γ a gamma: the rules read their means and covariances, and the mean and the mean of the logarithm of q(γ).\n\nThere are no belief-propagation rules, and other factorisations, such as a joint over θ and x, have no rules. There is an average energy for the two factorisations above.\n\nSee also: softdot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SoftDotMessagePassingRules.SoftDot"},{"category":"type","location":"#SoftDotMessagePassingRules.softdot","page":"Overview","text":"softdot\n\nAn alias of SoftDot, the node y ~ N(θᵀx, γ⁻¹).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SoftDotMessagePassingRules.softdot"}]
